
This is the main comparison you should remember:

|                       | REST                               | gRPC                   | GraphQL                               |
| --------------------- | ---------------------------------- | ---------------------- | ------------------------------------- |
| Style                 | Resource-based API                 | RPC                    | Query-based API                       |
| Think of              | `/users/101`                       | `getUser(101)`         | `user { name }`                       |
| Common data format    | JSON                               | Protobuf               | Usually JSON responses                |
| Transport             | Usually HTTP                       | HTTP/2                 | Usually HTTP                          |
| Client chooses fields | Limited/endpoint-defined           | Contract-defined       | ✅ Yes                                 |
| Human readable        | High                               | Lower on wire          | High                                  |
| Streaming             | Possible, but not core REST itself | ✅ Strong support       | Possible via subscriptions/transports |
| Common use            | Public/web APIs                    | Internal microservices | Flexible frontend APIs                |
| Pagination            | Offset/Cursor                      | Tokens/Cursor          | Offset/Cursor                         |


Remember them like this:

REST
"What RESOURCE do you want?"

GET /users/101
gRPC
"What METHOD do you want to call?"

getUser(101)
GraphQL
"What DATA/FIELDS exactly do you want?"

user {
    name
    email
}


!1. REST API!

REST (Representational State Transfer) is an architectural style used for communication between client and server, typically over HTTP.

REST APIs expose resources through URLs and use HTTP methods to perform operations on those resources.

Example:

GET    /users/101      → Get user
POST   /users          → Create user
PUT    /users/101      → Replace/update user
PATCH  /users/101      → Partially update user
DELETE /users/101      → Delete user

Data is commonly exchanged using JSON.

{
  "id": 101,
  "name": "Nishchay"
}
Important REST characteristics
Resource-based URLs
Usually HTTP/HTTPS
Stateless requests
Uses HTTP methods
Uses HTTP status codes
Commonly JSON
Simple and widely used
Pagination in REST

Suppose:

GET /products

There are 10 million products.

Returning all of them is inefficient, so we return smaller chunks.

#A. Offset Pagination

Client provides limit and offset.

GET /products?limit=20&offset=0

→ First 20 records

Next:

GET /products?limit=20&offset=20

→ Next 20 records

Think:

offset = how many records to skip
limit  = how many records to return

Advantages: Simple and supports jumping to arbitrary pages.

Disadvantages: Large offsets can become expensive, and inserts/deletes while paginating can cause duplicates or skipped records.



#B. Cursor Pagination

Instead of saying "skip N records", we say :

Give me records after this position.

First request:

GET /messages?limit=20

Response:

{
  "messages": ["..."],
  "nextCursor": "msg_120"
}

Next:

GET /messages?limit=20&cursor=msg_120

Conceptually:

Page 1

101 102 103 104 105
                ↑
              cursor

Page 2

106 107 108 109 110

Advantages: Efficient for large/changing datasets and good for infinite scrolling.

Disadvantage: Usually can't conveniently say "Take me directly to page 500."

REST pagination summary
Offset	Cursor
limit + offset	limit + cursor
Simple	Slightly complex
Jump to arbitrary page ✅	Usually ❌
Large offsets can be slow	Efficient
Changing data can cause issues	Better for changing data
Traditional pages	Feeds/chat/infinite scroll




!2. gRPC

gRPC = Remote Procedure Call framework.

It allows one service to call a function/method exposed by another service almost like calling a local function.

Order Service
      │
      │ getUser(101)
      ▼
 User Service

Rather than thinking:

GET /users/101

you can conceptually think:

userService.getUser(101)

gRPC commonly uses Protocol Buffers (Protobuf) to define service contracts and serialize data.

Example conceptually:

service UserService {

    GetUser(userId)
        → User
}
Important gRPC points
Commonly uses Protocol Buffers
Binary serialization
Runs over HTTP/2 in its standard implementation
Strongly typed contracts
Supports streaming
Efficient service-to-service communication
Popular in microservice architectures

Typical architecture:

             REST
Client ─────────────→ API Gateway
                         │
                         │ gRPC
                  ┌──────┼───────┐
                  ▼      ▼       ▼
                User   Order   Payment
               Service Service Service

REST is often convenient for public/client-facing APIs, while gRPC can be a strong option for internal service-to-service communication. This isn't a hard rule.

Pagination in gRPC

Pagination concept is basically the same.

For example, request:

ListProducts {
    page_size: 20
    page_token: "abc123"
}

Response:

products: [...]
next_page_token: "xyz456"

Then:

ListProducts {
    page_size: 20
    page_token: "xyz456"
}

So page_token acts like a cursor.

Important:

Pagination isn't fundamentally a REST feature. It's a data-retrieval technique.

REST, gRPC and GraphQL can all implement pagination.


!3. GraphQL

GraphQL is an API query language and runtime where the client can specify exactly which fields it wants.

Suppose a user contains:

id
name
email
phone
address
DOB
profilePicture
followers

But your UI only needs:

name
profilePicture

With GraphQL:

query {
  user(id: 101) {
    name
    profilePicture
  }
}

Response:

{
  "name": "Nishchay",
  "profilePicture": "..."
}

The client requested only the fields it needed.

Why GraphQL?

One major motivation is reducing over-fetching and making it easier for clients to fetch related data through a flexible schema.

Over-fetching: Server returns much more data than the client needs.

Under-fetching: Client needs multiple requests to gather the data required for one screen/use case.

GraphQL can help with both, although poorly designed GraphQL APIs can introduce their own performance issues.

Important GraphQL points
Client specifies required fields
Strongly typed schema
Typically exposed through a single HTTP endpoint
Helps with over-fetching/under-fetching
Flexible for frontend clients
Can make caching/query-cost control more complicated than straightforward REST APIs
Pagination in GraphQL

GraphQL can also use offset or cursor-based pagination.

Cursor pagination is very common.

A common style is:

query {
  users(first: 10, after: "cursor123") {
    edges {
      node {
        id
        name
      }
      cursor
    }

    pageInfo {
      endCursor
      hasNextPage
    }
  }
}

Don't memorize this syntax for HLD.

Just understand:

Give me first 10 records
        ↓
Server returns records
        +
endCursor
        +
hasNextPage
        ↓
Client sends endCursor
        ↓
Next 10 records

This is commonly known as cursor/connection-style pagination.

REST vs gRPC vs GraphQL


for pagination, don't think there are three completely different concepts.

                 PAGINATION
                     │
           ┌─────────┴─────────┐
           ▼                   ▼
        OFFSET              CURSOR
      Pagination           Pagination
           │                   │
           └─────────┬─────────┘
                     ▼
            Can be exposed via
                     │
          ┌──────────┼──────────┐
          ▼          ▼          ▼
        REST       gRPC      GraphQL

So for interviews, the core pagination concepts you really need to understand are Offset vs Cursor. REST/gRPC/GraphQL are simply different API approaches through which those pagination strategies can be exposed.